NAME
       3pack-patterns - triadic action patterns for RTT-aligned workflows

SYNOPSIS
       Patterns built from the core 3-Pack cycle:
           P1 (begin) → P2 (transform) → P3 (close)

DESCRIPTION
       The 3-Pack is the smallest complete RTT-aligned action. Higher-order
       patterns combine multiple triads to express structure, recursion,
       concurrency, symmetry, refinement, or distributed intent.

       This manual describes the major triadic patterns and when to use them.

CORE PATTERN
       core-3pack
              A single begin → transform → close cycle.
              Use for atomic, self-contained actions.

PATTERNS
       sequential
              Repeating triadic cycles.
              Use for pipelines, loops, rhythmic workflows.

       nested
              A triad inside a triad.
              Use when a step contains sub-steps or recursion.

       expansion (3x3)
              Each primitive becomes its own triad.
              Use for deep elaboration or multi-phase simulation.

       ladder
              Triads arranged in ascending refinement levels.
              Use for progressive abstraction or staged reasoning.

       mirror
              Forward triad followed by reverse triad.
              Use for reversible operations or symmetry.

       spiral
              Each cycle expands or deepens.
              Use for adaptive systems, iterative refinement, growth.

       constellation
              Multiple independent triads orbit a shared intent.
              Use for multi-agent or distributed coordination.

       weave
              Interleaved triads across threads or layers.
              Use for concurrency or braided workflows.

       cascade
              Each triad triggers the next.
              Use for staged pipelines or dependent processes.

       mutation
              Variation or branching inside P2.
              Use for evolutionary algorithms or exploratory transforms.

       lens
              A triad applied as a reframing overlay.
              Use for interpretive passes or perspective shifts.

DECISION GUIDE
       Use core-3pack if the action is simple.

       If it repeats, use sequential.
       If a step contains sub-steps, use nested.
       If each primitive needs elaboration, use expansion.
       If the workflow ascends levels, use ladder.
       If symmetry is required, use mirror.
       If cycles deepen or widen, use spiral.
       If multiple agents share intent, use constellation.
       If concurrency or interleaving is needed, use weave.
       If stages depend on each other, use cascade.
       If P2 varies or branches, use mutation.
       If the triad reframes another process, use lens.

